You are implementing a verified kernel fix in SuperGrok1.5 on a real H100 (sm_90). Working dir: /workspace/SuperGrok1.5. You will EDIT exactly one file and COMPILE-CHECK it. Do NOT rebuild the full _ops extension (no pip install / setup.py). Do NOT run training or the race.

FILE TO EDIT: grokking_optimizers/kernels/sm_90/supergrok2_sm90.cuh

CONTEXT: SuperGrok2's fused step crashes with `RuntimeError: shape '[-1, 44]' is invalid for input of size 192`. Root cause (already diagnosed + verified against two independent references): the function `peer_expert_forward` COLLAPSES all 4 PEER heads instead of looping over them. The PEER weights are stacked per-head: `peer_query_Ws` = [num_peer_heads=4, d_model=8, peer_in=22]; `prod_keys_A`/`prod_keys_B` = [4, pk_dim=12, d_model/2=4]. The buggy code flattens the head axis. Two canonical references AGREE on the correct per-head layout and you MUST match them:
  - csrc/algorithms/supergrok2_bilevel_adjoint.h, function peer_head_backward (~lines 610-707) and its driver loop (~751-765): `for (h=0; h<num_peer_heads; ++h)`, each Wq[h]=[d_model,peer_in], half=d_model/2, prod_A[h]/prod_B[h]=[pk_dim,half], expert-MLP input is per-element g, total /= num_peer_heads.
  - csrc/backends/pallas/launch_supergrok2.py, function peer_expert_forward (~lines 288-354): identical per-head structure.

STEP 0 — READ FIRST: Read supergrok2_sm90.cuh around the function `peer_expert_forward` (roughly lines 1409-1512) and its caller `csa_hca_step_one` (roughly lines 1517-1700, esp. the section ~1617-1624 that builds `feat` and calls peer_expert_forward). Read both canonical references above. Confirm the exact current variable names, the `run_experts` helper, the tensor-options aliases (lopt/fopt etc.), and the perm/sort handling. The line numbers are approximate — locate by symbol.

EDIT 1 (MANDATORY — the per-head loop). Replace the body of `peer_expert_forward` so it loops over heads. The verified target structure (adapt variable names to the actual code you read):
```cpp
    // Per-head PEER product-key routing (matches bilevel adjoint peer_head_backward
    // + Pallas peer_expert_forward). peer_query_Ws = [num_peer_heads, d_model, peer_in];
    // prod_keys_{A,B} = [num_peer_heads, pk_dim, d_model/2]. half = d_model/2 (NOT Q/2).
    // Each head routes independently; head outputs are averaged.
    torch::Tensor out = torch::zeros({N}, fopt32);   // fopt32 = float32 opts on this device
    std::vector<torch::Tensor> count_chunks;
    if (prod_keys_A.defined() && prod_keys_A.numel() > 0 &&
        peer_query_Ws.defined() && peer_query_Ws.dim() == 3) {
        const int num_peer_heads = static_cast<int>(peer_query_Ws.size(0));
        const int half = d_model / 2;
        const double T = 10.0;   // gate temperature (matches adjoint PEER head)
        const bool has_B = prod_keys_B.defined() && prod_keys_B.numel() > 0;
        for (int h = 0; h < num_peer_heads; ++h) {
            auto Wq = peer_query_Ws.select(0, h).to(torch::kFloat32);   // [d_model, peer_in]
            auto query = peer_input.matmul(Wq.transpose(0, 1));         // [N, d_model]
            auto A = prod_keys_A.select(0, h).to(torch::kFloat32);      // [pk_dim, half]
            const int na = static_cast<int>(A.size(0));
            auto qa = query.narrow(1, 0, half);
            auto sa = qa.matmul(A.transpose(0, 1));                     // [N, pk_dim]
            const int ka = std::min<int>(std::max(peer_topk, 1), na);
            auto ta = sa.topk(ka, -1);
            auto top_a_vals = std::get<0>(ta), top_a_idx = std::get<1>(ta);
            auto soft_a = torch::softmax(top_a_vals * T, -1);
            torch::Tensor expert_idx, routing_w;
            if (has_B && (d_model - half) > 0) {
                auto B = prod_keys_B.select(0, h).to(torch::kFloat32);  // [pk_dim, half]
                auto qb = query.narrow(1, half, d_model - half);
                auto sb = qb.matmul(B.transpose(0, 1));                 // [N, pk_dim]
                const int nb = static_cast<int>(B.size(0));
                const int kb = std::min<int>(std::max(peer_topk, 1), nb);
                auto tb = sb.topk(kb, -1);
                auto top_b_vals = std::get<0>(tb), top_b_idx = std::get<1>(tb);
                auto soft_b = torch::softmax(top_b_vals * T, -1);
                expert_idx = (top_a_idx.unsqueeze(2) * na + top_b_idx.unsqueeze(1))
                                 .reshape({N, ka * kb}).clamp(0, num_experts - 1);
                routing_w = (soft_a.unsqueeze(2) * soft_b.unsqueeze(1)).reshape({N, ka * kb});
            } else {
                expert_idx = top_a_idx.clamp(0, num_experts - 1);
                routing_w  = soft_a;
            }
            out = out + run_experts(expert_idx, routing_w);            // [N]
            count_chunks.push_back(expert_idx.reshape({-1}));
        }
        out = out / static_cast<float>(std::max(num_peer_heads, 1));
    } else {
        auto expert_idx = torch::zeros({N, 1}, lopt);                  // lopt = long opts
        auto routing_w  = torch::ones({N, 1}, fopt32);
        out = run_experts(expert_idx, routing_w);
        count_chunks.push_back(expert_idx.reshape({-1}));
    }
    auto count_idx = count_chunks.size() == 1 ? count_chunks[0] : torch::cat(count_chunks, 0);
```
ALSO: the function signature must change — `peer_expert_forward` must take `peer_input` ([N, peer_in=22]) and `g_elem` ([N], the per-element gradient) instead of the old single `feat`. The expert-MLP input inside `run_experts` must use `g_elem` (per-element grad), NOT `feat.mean(1)`. Find `run_experts`'s `scalar_in = feat.mean(1)` and change it to use `g_elem`. Define `fopt32` near the top: `auto fopt32 = torch::TensorOptions().dtype(torch::kFloat32).device(peer_input.device());`. Keep `lopt` as it currently is (long opts).

EDIT 2 (gru placeholder — break the cascade, make it RUN). In `csa_hca_step_one`, where it currently does `auto feat = csa_ctx + hca_ctx;` then calls peer_expert_forward, instead build the real-width peer_input. The eager net expects peer_input = cat(gru_new[N,gru_hidden=4], csa_ctx[N,d_model=8], hca_ctx[N,d_model=8], g[N,1], s[N,1]) = [N, 22], all SORTED by the same perm the csa/hca path uses. The fused forward currently has NO matrix-GRU (the gru weights are (void)'d and folded into a scalar mu_state blend). Per the audit decision, DO NOT reconstruct the matrix GRU now — use a PLACEHOLDER gru_new of zeros [N, gru_hidden] (gru_hidden = peer_input_dim - 2*d_model - 2; derive it, do not hardcode if a variable exists). Build:
```cpp
    auto g_sorted = /* the per-element grad, sorted by perm, as [N] float32 */;
    auto s_sorted = /* the per-element sharpness, sorted by perm, as [N] float32 */;
    const int gru_hidden = peer_input_dim - 2 * d_model - 2;  // or read the real var
    auto gru_new = torch::zeros({N, gru_hidden}, fopt32);     // PLACEHOLDER (audit-gated; see note)
    auto peer_input = torch::cat({gru_new, csa_ctx, hca_ctx,
                                  g_sorted.unsqueeze(1), s_sorted.unsqueeze(1)}, 1);  // [N, 22]
    auto expert_sorted = detail::peer_expert_forward(
        peer_input, g_sorted, peer_query_Ws, prod_keys_A, prod_keys_B,
        /* ...expert tensors... */, N, d_model, num_experts, expert_hidden,
        expert_counts, /*peer_topk=*/peer_topk);
```
Add a clear comment: `// NOTE: gru_new is a zero placeholder — the fused path does not reconstruct the matrix GRU (deliberate simplification). Expert-selection match vs the eager net is validated downstream; if selection diverges, reconstruct the matrix-GRU forward here.`
You must check the REAL variable name for peer_input_dim / gru_hidden / d_model and the perm used to sort csa_ctx/hca_ctx, and sort g/s by the SAME perm so ordering is consistent. Confirm csa_ctx/hca_ctx are in sorted order at this point (the diagnosis says they are).

DO NOT TOUCH: the input_proj dtype handling (~lines 1568-1592) — it's a prior fix, leave intact. Update the stale signature comment (`// [num_heads?, d_model] or [d_model]`) to the correct `[num_peer_heads, d_model, peer_in]`.

COMPILE-CHECK (the only build you run): after editing, verify the TU compiles:
  `bash scripts/compile_to_object.sh csrc/backends/cuda/sm_90/launch_supergrok2.cu -DWITH_CUTLASS 2>&1 | tail -30`
(this machine has 1.5TB RAM so it won't OOM). Iterate on compile errors until it reports COMPILE_OK. If a different TU is the right one to compile-check for this kernel, find it (grep which .cu includes supergrok2_sm90.cuh). Resolve any type/shape/option mismatches you introduced.

DELIVERABLE (return structured text):
- The exact edits you made (file:line ranges + what changed), as a unified summary.
- The final COMPILE result (paste the COMPILE_OK / error tail).
- Any deviation from the spec above and why (e.g. a variable was named differently, or run_experts needed a different change).
- A precise description of HOW to measure expert-selection match vs the eager net after the .so is rebuilt (which Python call produces the fused expert_idx, and which eager net method produces the reference expert_idx), so the main thread can run that gate.
- Residual risks / what you could NOT verify without the full rebuild.
Be meticulous — this is a complex kernel on a production H100 path. Match the canonical references exactly.

