1397	// REAL product-key top-k routing (restored): the query projection is split into
1398	// halves q_a / q_b; we score against product-key sub-codebooks A and B, take the
1399	// top-k of EACH (k = peer_topk, default 4), form the k×k Cartesian product of
1400	// candidate experts (expert = a_idx * nb + b_idx), softmax-weight them by the
1401	// summed sub-scores, run the per-element expert MLP for each of the k² selected
1402	// experts and return the routing-weighted combination. This matches the bilevel
1403	// adjoint's PEER head (csrc/algorithms/supergrok2_bilevel_adjoint.h:
1404	// peer_head_backward) which uses scores_a.topk / scores_b.topk + softmax over
1405	// num_active = topk*topk. The previous code collapsed this to argmax (top-1) and
1406	// gathered a single expert — a routing-quality regression vs the trained
1407	// top-k product-key gate. When product keys are absent we fall back to a single
1408	// shared expert (index 0), unchanged.
1409	static torch::Tensor peer_expert_forward(
1410	    const torch::Tensor& feat,              // [N, d_model] float (gru ⊕ ctx)
1411	    const torch::Tensor& peer_query_Ws,     // [num_heads?, d_model] or [d_model]
